TESTING:

 In order to test my program, the MagicSquareTest driver,
 valid squares, and invalid squares were used. I also
 generated 7 x 7 and 9 x 9 squares and used both the program
 and manual validation (as in, checking each row, column,
 and diagonal all equaled the "magic number") to ensure the
 output was correct.

 It was with both the MagicSquareTest driver and manual
 checking that I noticed that the provided squares used
 either spaces or tabs between each value. This forced me to
 rework my understanding of how the files were formatted and
 how to handle each, which the Java documentation thankfully
 helped answer: when scanning between each value, there is a
 special RegEx combination (\\s+) which, when used in
 combination with the corresponding Scanner object's
 useDelimiter() method, can handle all whitespace (blank
 characters).

 In order to handle bad input, the program prints an error
 specifying the problem and prints the usage statements. Bad
 input could mean a variety of things, but in this case it
 primarily means a lack of arguments, bad flags, or the
 specified size being invalid. It also means the program will
 detect and exit if the specified filename from the command-
 line cannot be read or written to. To my knowledge, there are
 no edge cases where a possible error isn't handled manually,
 or the program will exit unexpectedly and print a stack trace.

 This program is certainly programmer-proof, but you can't
 trust programs which proclaim to be idiot-proof. You can only
 misuse something so hard, but the limits to that misuse is
 hard to gauge. This is about as good as it gets in terms of
 idiot-proofing, and I know this because I tested a very wide
 variety of possible argument combinations and errors, and
 caught all I was instructed to catch and all that were not
 mentioned in the project description.

 Currently, there is *technically* only one known "issue," and
 it involves how the rows in matrixArray are iterated through
 in the toString() method, which uses a simple for loop instead
 of a collection loop. This is only a technical problem because
 it means the program will run microseconds slower, and I could
 not care less about optimizing a command-line program if its
 functionality is not harmed by its current state of being. This
 "optimization" is not only unnecessary but risks sacrificing
 readability.


